  "image_name": "0d0c26d9a95a323d761a47da725a3d20.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic of the molecular dialogue between a T cell and a dendritic cell (DC), highlighting how immune activation is balanced by inhibitory “checkpoint” signals. The two cells are shown face to face, with paired receptors and ligands spanning the contact zone. \n\nA central activating interaction is the T-cell receptor binding MHC on the DC. Around this, several co-stimulatory pathways promote T-cell activation, including CD28 with CD80/CD86, CD40L with CD40, OX40 with OX40L, 4-1BB with 4-1BBL, ICOS with ICOSL, and CD27 with CD70. These are indicated with green arrows, suggesting positive signaling into the T cell.\n\nIn contrast, multiple inhibitory receptors on the T cell are shown with red inhibitory marks, emphasizing immune suppression or restraint. These include BTLA–HVEM, GITR–GITRL, CTLA4–CD80/CD86, TIGIT–CD155, PD1 binding PDL1 or PDL2, TIM3–Galectin 9, and LAG3. PD1 appears twice, reflecting its interaction with both PDL1 and PDL2. One additional interaction is marked with a question mark, implying uncertainty or incomplete understanding.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that T-cell responses are controlled by a complex mixture of stimulatory and inhibitory receptor-ligand pairs at the immune synapse.",
